# v28 (2026-08-03) — BEHAVIOUR FIX in this file (docs/kit-test-suite-spec.md, "Defect 7", found
# 2026-08-01): `register` was fail-open on the one control the rest of the gate assumes. The
# contract-validator guard and the registered-manifest.sha256 hash-lock guard both gated on
# manifest_schema_version == "level3-operational-v2", and neither had an else branch. Hand register
# a manifest without that field — the tracked authored contract under docs/autopilot-manifests/ is
# FORBIDDEN from carrying it — and both guards fell through: no validation ran, no hash-lock was
# written, INFLIGHT.md still got a real in-flight reservation, and "registered: ... (in-flight)"
# printed unconditionally. The writer had every reason to believe the slice was locked; it was not.
# `ready` later halted MANIFEST_DRIFT ("manifest changed after registration") for a manifest that
# was never locked in the first place, so the debugging trail pointed at a change that never
# happened.
# Fix shape: the property, not the two conditionals. `register` now HALTs CONTRACT_INVALID up
# front — before acquire()/append_block() ever touch INFLIGHT.md, before TASK_STATE is created —
# naming the missing field and the exact runtime path (.autopilot/state/<feature>/manifest.json)
# the manifest should have been. Checking this before any mutation, not only at the hash-lock site,
# is the part that matters: a patch that only added the missing else branch there would still let
# acquire()/append_block() write a real INFLIGHT.md reservation first, so the halted attempt would
# leave a dangling in-flight block that a corrected retry then collides with
# (REJECT FEATURE_ALREADY_ACTIVE) — trading a silent lock failure for a locked-out legitimate retry.
# Verified test-first on a scratch repo (test/e2e.sh, "defect7" section): RED against the pre-fix
# code (register exited 0, no hash file, a dangling INFLIGHT.md reservation, no HALT), GREEN after
# (a manifest without manifest_schema_version HALTs with no hash file and no INFLIGHT.md entry; the
# real runtime manifest still registers and its hash-lock matches).
# BREAKING CHANGE, deliberately: every consumer repo's per-slice launcher that calls
# `register --manifest docs/autopilot-manifests/<slug>.json` (the authored path) now HALTs instead
# of silently succeeding unlocked. That is the correct outcome — those registrations were never
# actually locked — but it lands immediately on upgrade, not staged. Fixing those launchers is
# explicitly out of scope for this fix; most belong to already-merged slices, and with the tool
# halting, updating them becomes ordinary cleanup rather than a defence anyone is relying on.
# v27 (2026-08-01) — ap-watch.sh's MAX_ROUNDS ceiling (MAXR_HALT, hard-coded default 6) sat one round
# above the ceiling this kit actually enforces. The readiness gate's own manifest field,
# budgets.fix_rounds_max (autopilot-review-readiness.mjs:377, manifests/_TEMPLATE.json:56), is 5, and a
# real terminal HALT stops there, not at 6: backlog-level3-reconcile-v3's own HALT-MAX_ROUNDS.txt reads
# "round 5 (the founder-declared last permitted round, fix_rounds_max=5) returned 5 actionable
# findings ... no round 6 fix attempt was made" (fix-round-count.txt=5 on disk). With MAXR_HALT=6, the
# pre-thrash warning ("fix-round-count=4 (HALT khi ≥6)") promised two rounds of headroom when only one
# remained. The identical misleading line was logged five separate times (zalo-player-join-gate-on,
# backlog-level3-reconcile-v3, messenger-player-join-gate-on, identity-claim-authz-hardening-v3,
# identity-claim-authz-gate-gen2), per docs/autopilot-backlog.md's own
# `ap-watch-max-rounds-ceiling-off-by-one` entry (filed 2026-08-01, the same day, by a session that had
# just synced to this same KIT_VERSION 26).
# Fix shape: do not restate the number as a second literal (6, or a corrected-but-still-hardcoded 5) —
# read it. ap-watch.sh now derives the ceiling PER SLICE from that slice's own runtime manifest.json
# (budgets.fix_rounds_max), via a portable python3/node/jq reader (read_fix_rounds_max, mirroring this
# file's own json_get() pattern), falling back to 5 only when a manifest is missing, unreadable, or
# carries a value outside the 0..5 schema range. MAXR_WARN is now derived as maxr_halt-1 ("warn one
# round before the ceiling") instead of an independent constant, so the two numbers cannot drift apart
# from each other the way MAXR_HALT already drifted from fix_rounds_max.
# Same failure shape as v23 (BASE_STALE, one rule kept in two copies) and v24 (registry-lookup, one bug
# kept in four copies across three files): a number duplicated instead of read eventually disagrees
# with itself.
# Per WATCHDOG-NOTES.md's own closing rule, ap-watch's alert paths — including this one — are outside
# test-level3-operational-kit.sh's coverage (it drives only the resolver + `bash -n`; the alert paths
# need osascript/stat -f/lsof/pgrep). This fix is verified by reading — bash -n, a standalone
# read_fix_rounds_max test against valid/missing/malformed/out-of-range manifests, and re-derivation
# against autopilot-review-readiness.mjs and a real HALT-MAX_ROUNDS.txt — not by CI.
# v26 (2026-07-31) — v25's own note overclaimed: "the diff handed to the reviewer... is now taken from
# the LIVE base-ref tip" was true only for the internal SCOPE_DRIFT/diffHash computation inside
# liveDiffState(). Four other sites still read the pinned base_sha for the same window job: the --base
# flag actually handed to the reviewer process, the two prompt strings describing that base to it, and
# ap-finish.sh's own recomputed diff hash and file list. Measured consequences: the reviewer reviewed
# 11 files for a slice that changed 5 (told --base <stale pin>, not the live window), and a nominally
# PASSING gate produced a diff hash ap-finish could never reproduce, because ap-finish recomputed from
# base_sha while the gate's own diff_files/diff_sha256 had already moved to the live base-ref tip.
# Fix shape: one source, not two. liveDiffState() now returns diffBase alongside its other fields;
# every reviewer invocation — the --base flag and both prompt strings — reads initialState.diffBase
# instead of the module-level baseSha; gate-result.json gains a diff_base_sha field recording exactly
# what the gate used. ap-finish.sh reads that field and recomputes its live_diff_hash and feature_files
# from it — no fallback to base_sha, because a fallback is a second source of truth, which is the
# defect this closes. base_sha itself is untouched everywhere it does its actual job: assertBaseUsable
# ()'s legitimacy check, the register-time HEAD==base_sha equality, BASE_DRIFT, dependency-landed-
# before-base proofs and gate-result.json's own base_sha field — kept alongside diff_base_sha, not
# replaced by it, because legitimacy and window are still two different questions, just no longer
# answered by the same value.
# Also closes three defects in autopilot-backlog-reconcile's close_by/scope guard logic, found the
# same day by the same live slice's Codex review rounds — see that file's own v7 note.
# v25 (2026-07-31) — the root the last three fixes were circling: `base_sha` was doing TWO jobs. One is
# proving the review rests on a legitimate base — v21/v23 moved that to assertBaseUsable(), which checks
# ancestry plus untouched scope. The other is marking where the slice's own work begins, and for THAT the
# pinned SHA is simply wrong the moment anything lands on the base branch. A slice that rebases to pick
# up a gate fix finds every intervening commit inside `base_sha...HEAD`, and SCOPE_DRIFT then reports
# files it never touched. Measured: a slice rebased through three sanctioned kit syncs and halted on four
# kit files with no legal way out — re-registering is refused while in-flight (FEATURE_ALREADY_ACTIVE)
# and editing the runtime manifest breaks its hash lock (MANIFEST_DRIFT). The registration was correct;
# the measurement was not.
# The diff window — commit count, changed-file list, and the diff handed to the reviewer — is now taken
# from the LIVE base-ref tip. Correct in both states: rebased, merge-base is that tip so the window is
# exactly this slice's commits; not rebased, merge-base is the old divergence point, which is also
# exactly this slice's commits. The pinned SHA was right in neither case once the branch moved.
# Pattern worth naming, and it now has three instances in one day: BASE_STALE twice, then SCOPE_DRIFT,
# were three symptoms of one overloaded field, and fixing symptoms in sequence produced two of them.
# Ask what a value MEANS before fixing where it is compared. The v24 note below is the same lesson in the
# other direction — one meaning, copied into four places.
# v24 (2026-07-31) — the v20 registry-lookup bug had FOUR copies across THREE files. v20 fixed the two
# inside this file, because that is where the search was run. The other two — ap-finish.sh
# registry_status() and ap-housekeep.sh — kept the reset, so `ap-finish.sh --squash-verified` refused
# every feature that was not the last block in the registry, reporting that the status must be `ready`
# while the registry plainly said `ready`. The housekeeping inventory silently listed live reservations
# as `absent` for the same reason. Both fixed; a repo-wide search now finds no remaining copy.
# This is the second time in one day that a fix landed in some copies of a rule and not all of them
# (see v23). The pattern in both: search inside the file already open, treat that result as the whole
# set. When changing a rule, enumerate its copies ACROSS THE PACK before editing any of them, then
# collapse them into one definition so there is nothing left to enumerate next time.
# v23 (2026-07-31) — v21 fixed one of TWO copies of the BASE_STALE rule. The pre-flight copy got the new
# ancestry+scope logic; a second copy inside liveDiffState() — the one `ready` actually passes through —
# kept exact equality. So the fix looked landed, was verified in the file, and changed nothing for the
# slice that needed it; the only clue was that the halt message did not match either new condition.
# Both call sites now share ONE function, assertBaseUsable(). The rule has a single definition, so the
# next change to it cannot land in half the places.
# How the duplicate survived v21: the author searched for BASE_STALE and piped the result through
# `head`, then read the truncated output as the complete set. Truncated output is not exhaustive output —
# the same failure shape as reading an empty query result as an empty world, which this kit already
# records. When enumerating call sites of a rule you are about to change, print all of them.
# v22 (2026-07-31) — two findings from the first codex round to reach the close_by logic. One was a real
# bug, one was a doc/code mismatch resolved on the doc side.
# (1) REAL: the close_by abuse guard tested file EXTENSION rather than config-vs-source, so
# eslint.config.mjs read as source and the guard refused a live dependency slice that has nothing
# symbol-like to probe — the same category as pnpm-workspace.yaml, already allowed. Config filenames
# (`*.config.*`, dotfile rc files, lockfiles) now join docs, data and manifests on the allowed side;
# real source and bare scripts stay refused.
# (2) DOC, NOT CODE: the reviewer flagged that `is_wellformed_close_by` never enforced the `·` the
# documentation showed. Measured: it accepts `hand <reason>` with any separator and rejects `hand` alone
# — which is the behaviour worth having. Enforcing one hard-to-type character protects nothing and
# invents a fresh class of authoring error, so the docs and the failure message were corrected to say the
# reason is required and the interpunct is convention. The mismatch was real; the wrong side was written
# down.
# Worth recording about how (1) survived: the author of this helper DID test it, with fixtures for the
# cases they had in mind rather than the cases the contract distinguishes. `hand <reason without a
# middot>` was never among them, and it was the exact shape in question. A test suite written from the
# same mental model that produced the code inherits that model's blind spots; the codex round did not.
# v21 (2026-07-31) — BEHAVIOUR FIX in autopilot-review-readiness.mjs: BASE_STALE no longer requires
# base_sha to EQUAL the tip of base_ref. It now requires base_sha to remain an ANCESTOR of that tip, and
# no file in the slice's declared `scope` to have changed on base_ref since. Exact equality made every
# concurrent registration terminal the moment anything landed on the default branch — an unrelated docs
# commit was enough — so the parallel model this kit documents could never complete: the first slice to
# merge killed every other in-flight slice. Measured 2026-07-31 with three lanes live; all three went
# BASE_STALE from a single chore commit, and the symptom read as a registry fault rather than as this
# check. The two replacement conditions preserve what mattered: a rewritten history still halts, and a
# competing writer touching this slice's own files still halts. What is no longer fatal is somebody
# touching an unrelated file. Division of labour, now explicit: this gate certifies the DIFF the
# reviewer read; ap-merge.sh certifies the MERGE by performing a real one and stopping on conflict.
# Verified before implementing, on a scratch repository, across three cases: unrelated commit on the
# base ref (was HALT, now PASS), in-scope file changed on the base ref (HALT, unchanged), and rewritten
# history so the base is no longer an ancestor (HALT, unchanged).
# v20 (2026-07-31) — BEHAVIOUR FIX in this file, and it is a parallelism killer. The `ready` lifecycle
# lookup read a feature's status out of INFLIGHT.md with an awk that reset its accumulator on EVERY
# `## feature:` header. The status was therefore only retrievable when the feature happened to be the
# LAST block in the registry; for any earlier block the captured value was erased by the next header and
# the gate rejected a perfectly valid lifecycle with
# `REJECT READY_LIFECYCLE_INVALID: status is 'absent'`.
# Latent for as long as one slice ran at a time — the single active feature was always last. It surfaced
# the moment three slices ran in parallel: the newest could run `ready`, the two before it could not, and
# the symptom read like registry corruption rather than a query bug. Both `ready` lookups fixed by
# dropping the reset. The per-block scanner near the top of this file KEEPS its reset — it evaluates each
# block in turn instead of accumulating one named feature, so there the reset is required. Same idiom,
# opposite requirement, which is exactly why the wrong one was easy to copy.
# Known and deliberately NOT fixed here: these two lookups do not skip fenced code blocks, while other
# INFLIGHT readers in this file do. No registry today contains a fence, and widening this change would
# make a behaviour fix carry unrelated hardening. Separate slice.
# v19 (2026-07-30) — the pack now carries the two docs that govern agent BEHAVIOUR, not just the scripts
# that enforce it: autopilot-prompt-GENERIC.md and level3-workflow.md, both into docs/autopilot-templates/.
# They were never in the pack. Each consumer held a hand-copy, and hand-copies drift: Plexco's
# autopilot-prompt-GENERIC.md was a day stale against canonical while a sibling file in the same
# directory had been updated that morning, and the task to build a mirror had sat unstarted in that
# repo's own backlog. Every rule this kit has learned the hard way — findings must be closable within the
# change set, never edit what you feed a gate, read the validator before authoring a field — reached
# exactly one repo until now. Also adds two rules worth propagating on their own: a precedence order for
# resolving trade-offs autonomously (fail-closed and least-privilege outrank everything, deadline never
# outvotes them), and the test for whether a question belongs to the owner at all — it does only when the
# answer is not derivable from the contract. Those two exist because a slice stopped to ask which version
# to use when its own manifest had already pre-authorized the answer.
# copy_pack now mkdir -p's the destination directory; a consumer without docs/autopilot-templates/ would
# otherwise fail the copy.
# v18 (2026-07-30) — pack content change, no behaviour change in THIS file. Fixes a defect this kit
# introduced in v15. `manifests/_TEMPLATE.json`'s `_verification_rule` called the entries "commands"
# and then illustrated every one of them with an English sentence ("osv-scanner reports zero findings
# for <pkg>"). runVerificationGroup() spawns each string as a shell command and gates on exit status,
# so prose fails on the first attempt and every attempt after. The examples taught louder than the
# wording: the author of the very next slice copied their shape and the gate rejected all of them at
# register time (Plexco authorize-tenant-action-core, 2026-07-30) — the writer had to rewrite them
# in-flight. Examples are now executable. Also records the adjacent lesson: some properties belong in
# `scope` rather than in a shell test — "no new migration" is already enforced by SCOPE_DRIFT when no
# migration path is declared, and writing a grep for it duplicates a structural guarantee.
# Second lesson from the same slice, recorded in level3-workflow.md rather than here: invariant tokens
# must be read out of the catalog before they are written into a manifest. Two plausible-sounding
# tokens were invented, both absent from the catalog, and would have HALTed INVARIANT_UNKNOWN. The
# catalog's machine block stores `token — description` lines with an em-dash and NO backticks, so a
# grep written for backticked tokens silently returns nothing — which reads exactly like an empty
# catalog instead of a wrong query.
# v17 (2026-07-30) — FIRST behaviour change to the pinned reviewer contract (codex-review-pin.sh 1→2).
# The pinned prompt bounded what to REVIEW but never bounded what could count as a BLOCKING finding: it
# says "review only the change set", then "inspect live source", then "CLEAN only when there are zero
# actionable findings". A reviewer following those instructions exactly will find repo-wide staleness —
# correctly — and block on it. But a writer cannot edit outside its registered scope, so that verdict is
# unsatisfiable and the slice loops until a breaker fires. Measured on Plexco deps-security-bump: three
# registrations, six fix rounds, three of them (2, 4, 6) the same class of out-of-scope docs finding, each
# round producing a better EXPLANATION of an unclosable gap. The REGION_THRASH breaker never fired because
# the writer explained itself in a different file each round, so `file:region` kept changing. The
# dependency change itself passed review clean three times. Fix: findings must be closable by editing the
# change set; everything else goes under "ADVISORY (outside change set)" and is excluded from the verdict.
# This preserves the reviewer's genuinely useful repo-wide observations without letting them deadlock a
# scoped slice. Consumers on kit <17 keep the old blocking behaviour until they sync.
# v16 (2026-07-30) — pack content change, no behaviour change in THIS file. Two fixes from one live
# slice. (1) `claude-notify-hook.sh` no longer writes its forensic sentinel as `AWAIT-FOUNDER-notify.txt`.
# That name sat inside the readiness gate's terminal-signal namespace, so every Claude Code permission
# prompt during a slice silently planted a fake terminal signal and the next `ready` refused to run with
# TERMINAL_CONFLICT; worse, the file's own text reads "Claude needs your permission", so deleting it was
# indistinguishable from bypassing the permission system and the agent was correctly denied for trying.
# The sentinel had no consumer at all — `ap-watch.sh` never scanned for it. Renamed to
# `notify-sentinel.txt`. (2) `level3-workflow.md` section 8 records a third measured gate gap: reviewer
# auth-TIER failures are classified terminal instead of resumable. The auth detector matches only
# 401-shaped strings, so a 400 `invalid_request_error` naming an unsupported model for the account type
# falls through to HALT REVIEW_UNAVAILABLE — demanding a full close-and-re-register for what is, in
# substance, exactly the CODEX_AUTH_REQUIRED "re-authenticate, then resume" condition. Observed after a
# mid-run operator re-auth, on a slice that had already completed three clean review rounds that day.
# v15 (2026-07-30) — pack content change, no behaviour change in THIS file. `manifests/_TEMPLATE.json`
# gains `_verification_rule` and `_scope_rule`. The first exists because the runtime manifest is
# hash-locked at `register`, so a pinned literal in `verification` cannot be corrected in place — it
# forces a full close-and-re-register cycle. A Plexco slice paid that cost: `verification.focused`
# asserted `postcss == 8.5.18`, the owner later narrowed the slice's scope, and the third registration
# followed. A sibling manifest pinned a test-count baseline that went stale within hours of authoring
# and HALTed its slice at MAX_ROUNDS. Verification must assert properties, never literals. The second
# rule restates that `scope` takes exact file paths, since a directory entry is rejected and the error
# arrives only at register time. See also level3-workflow.md section 8, which now records two measured,
# unresolved gaps in the gate (reviewer receives no scope; breaker counts infra flakes as fix rounds).
# v14 (2026-07-30) — pack content change, no behaviour change in THIS file. `autopilot-backlog-reconcile`
# v14 (2026-07-30) — pack content change, no behaviour change in THIS file. `autopilot-backlog-reconcile`
# (its own header goes 3 → 4) stops hard-coding `DEFAULT_BRANCH="main"`. It now resolves origin's default
# branch from refs/remotes/origin/HEAD, falls back to whichever single conventional name exists, honours
# AUTOPILOT_DEFAULT_BRANCH, and **HALTs** when it cannot resolve. Previously, in a `master`-default
# consumer the probe ref did not exist, `git grep ... 2>/dev/null` failed silently, every task reported
# "genuinely pending", and `--check` returned "No stale entries" exit 0 while checking nothing — the
# anti-rot mechanism was a silent no-op. A gate that cannot fail is not a gate.
# v13 (2026-07-30) — pack content change, no behaviour change in THIS file. `manifests/_TEMPLATE.json` gains a
# leading `_role` field stating that it is the RUNTIME manifest shape for
# `.autopilot/state/<FEATURE_ID>/manifest.json`, not the schema of the tracked authored contract under
# `docs/autopilot-manifests/`. That distinction was guessed wrong in practice: the template ships with
# `manifest_schema_version: level3-operational-v2` while living in the authored-contract directory, which
# reads as "your tracked manifests are an old version" when in fact adding that string to a tracked contract
# makes `register` HALT CONTRACT_INVALID (autopilot-review-readiness.mjs pins the path). See
# level3-workflow.md section 11.
# v12 (2026-07-29) — pack content change, no behaviour change in THIS file. Bumped because
# `autopilot-update.sh` derives the whole pack's canonical version from this line, and v12 adds
# `autopilot-backlog-reconcile` to the pack (it was shipped in the kit but never synced, so consumer
# fixes to it were invisible and permanent) plus an explicit `node:process` import in
# `autopilot-review-readiness.mjs` (bare `process` made `pnpm lint` red in any consumer that lints
# scripts/). Consumers at v11 auto-upgrade; do not hand-edit pack files to match.
# autopilot-scope-gate — parallel autopilot coordination (SCOPE_COLLISION gate).
# REPO-AGNOSTIC template. Copy to <repo>/scripts/autopilot-scope-gate, chmod +x, commit.
#
#   scripts/autopilot-scope-gate register --manifest docs/autopilot-manifests/<feature>.json
#   scripts/autopilot-scope-gate ready    --manifest <manifest>
#   scripts/autopilot-scope-gate cleanup  --feature <slug>  # internal to ap-finish; requires record
#
# Lets MULTIPLE autopilot/Claude-Code sessions run in parallel (one git worktree each) without
# clobbering each other: PARALLEL when file-scope + domain invariants are disjoint, SEQUENTIAL when
# they overlap. Owns the SHARED runtime registry at MAIN-repo-root/.autopilot/INFLIGHT.md (resolved
# via git-common-dir so every worktree hits the SAME file). Atomic across concurrent sessions via a
# portable mkdir-lock (macOS has no flock). JSON parsed with python3 → node → jq (whichever exists).
#
# register HALTs if:
#   - INVARIANT_UNKNOWN : a manifest invariant is not a canonical token in the repo catalog
#                         (docs/autopilot-invariant-catalog.md). Prevents synonym drift bypassing (b).
#   - DEP_MISSING       : a dependency is active or lacks a unique landing proof on the pinned base.
#   - SCOPE_COLLISION   : my scope files OR invariants intersect another ACTIVE feature.
# On PASS it replaces any existing block for my feature (e.g. queued) with a fresh in-flight block.
# ready  : run the repo review-readiness gate, then flip status → ready (keeps the reservation).
# cleanup: remove my block only after ap-finish wrote a valid landing/disposition record.
#
# NOTE (dependency semantics): absence from the runtime registry is never landing proof.
# `level3-operational-v2` manifests require one `dep_checks` entry per dependency, binding the
# dependency base, landed SHA and a slice-unique symbol to the downstream pinned base. The readiness
# validator enforces that proof; this shell layer also rejects active prerequisites and performs a
# quick merged-interface presence check.
set -euo pipefail

cmd="${1:-}"; shift || true
manifest=""; feature_arg=""
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --manifest) manifest="${2:-}"; shift 2 ;;
    --feature)  feature_arg="${2:-}"; shift 2 ;;
    *) echo "unknown arg: $1" >&2; exit 2 ;;
  esac
done
# cleanup may target a feature by slug (manifest is often deleted after merge); every other path
# needs a manifest. register/ready by --feature is not allowed (they need scope/invariants).
if [ "$cmd" = "cleanup" ] && [ -n "$feature_arg" ]; then
  :
else
  [ -n "$cmd" ] && [ -n "$manifest" ] || { echo "usage: autopilot-scope-gate <register|ready|cleanup> --manifest <path>   (or: cleanup --feature <slug>)" >&2; exit 2; }
fi

# --- shared registry at MAIN repo root (works from any worktree) ---
ROOT="$(cd "$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)" && cd .. && pwd -P)"
WORKTREE_ROOT="$(cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" && pwd -P)"
DIR="$ROOT/.autopilot"; INFLIGHT="$DIR/INFLIGHT.md"; LOCK="$DIR/INFLIGHT.lock"
mkdir -p "$DIR"
TASK_STATE_CANDIDATE=""
REGISTRY_LOCK_HELD=0
READY_LOCK=""
READY_LOCK_HELD=0

cleanup_locks(){
  if [ "$REGISTRY_LOCK_HELD" = 1 ]; then rmdir "$LOCK" 2>/dev/null || true; fi
  if [ "$READY_LOCK_HELD" = 1 ] && [ -n "$READY_LOCK" ]; then rmdir "$READY_LOCK" 2>/dev/null || true; fi
}
trap cleanup_locks EXIT

halt_gate(){
  _halt_code="$1"; shift
  _halt_message="$*"
  if [ -n "$TASK_STATE_CANDIDATE" ]; then
    mkdir -p "$TASK_STATE_CANDIDATE"
    _halt_file="$TASK_STATE_CANDIDATE/HALT-$_halt_code.txt"
    if [ ! -e "$_halt_file" ]; then
      {
        printf 'HALT %s: %s\n' "$_halt_code" "$_halt_message"
        printf 'created_at_utc: %s\n' "$(date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ')"
        printf '%s\n' 'resolution: close this terminal lifecycle and create a fresh successor task after fixing the cause'
      } > "$_halt_file"
    fi
  fi
  echo "HALT $_halt_code: $_halt_message" >&2
  exit 1
}
# INFLIGHT initialisation happens inside the lock (register/ready/cleanup) to avoid
# a race where two concurrent fresh-repo runs both see the file absent and clobber each other.

# --- portable JSON reader: scalar -> one line; array -> one item per line ---
json_get(){ # $1 = abs json path, $2 = key
  if command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    python3 - "$1" "$2" <<'PY'
import json,sys
try: d=json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))
except Exception as e: sys.stderr.write("HALT: invalid JSON %s: %s\n"%(sys.argv[1],e)); sys.exit(3)
v=d.get(sys.argv[2])
print("\n".join(map(str,v)) if isinstance(v,list) else ("" if v is None else str(v)))
PY
  elif command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    node -e 'const m=require(process.argv[1]);const v=m[process.argv[2]];if(Array.isArray(v))console.log(v.join("\n"));else if(v!=null)console.log(v);' "$1" "$2"
  elif command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    jq -r --arg k "$2" 'if (.[$k]|type)=="array" then .[$k][] else (.[$k]//empty) end' "$1"
  else
    echo "HALT: need python3, node, or jq to parse manifest JSON" >&2; return 1
  fi
}

# --- portable reader for dep_checks: array of {file,symbol} -> "file<TAB>symbol" per line ---
json_get_dep_checks(){ # $1 = abs json path
  if command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    python3 - "$1" <<'PY'
import json,sys
try: d=json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))
except Exception as e: sys.stderr.write("HALT: invalid JSON %s: %s\n"%(sys.argv[1],e)); sys.exit(3)
for c in (d.get("dep_checks") or []):
    f=str(c.get("file","")).strip(); s=str(c.get("symbol","")).strip()
    if f and s: print("%s\t%s"%(f,s))
PY
  elif command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    node -e 'const m=require(process.argv[1]);for(const c of (m.dep_checks||[])){if(c&&c.file&&c.symbol)console.log(c.file+"\t"+c.symbol);}' "$1"
  elif command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    jq -r '(.dep_checks // [])[] | select(.file and .symbol) | "\(.file)\t\(.symbol)"' "$1"
  else
    echo "HALT: need python3, node, or jq to parse manifest JSON" >&2; return 1
  fi
}

# --- manifest fields — skipped for cleanup-by-slug ---
if [ -n "$manifest" ]; then
  _manifest_input_dir="$(dirname "$manifest")"
  if [ ! -d "$_manifest_input_dir" ]; then
    echo "HALT MANIFEST_MISSING: parent directory does not exist for $manifest" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  MANABS="$(cd "$_manifest_input_dir" && pwd -P)/$(basename "$manifest")"
  _manifest_state_dir="$(dirname "$MANABS")"
  _manifest_state_parent="$(dirname "$_manifest_state_dir")"
  _manifest_state_feature="$(basename "$_manifest_state_dir")"
  if [ "$_manifest_state_parent" = "$WORKTREE_ROOT/.autopilot/state" ]; then
    case "$_manifest_state_feature" in
      ""|[!a-z0-9]*|*[!a-z0-9._-]*) ;;
      *) TASK_STATE_CANDIDATE="$_manifest_state_dir" ;;
    esac
  fi
  [ -f "$MANABS" ] || halt_gate MANIFEST_MISSING "$MANABS"
  if ! json_get "$MANABS" feature >/dev/null; then
    halt_gate MANIFEST_INVALID "cannot parse $MANABS with an available JSON reader"
  fi
  FEATURE="$(json_get "$MANABS" feature)"; BRANCH="$(json_get "$MANABS" branch)"; RISK="$(json_get "$MANABS" risk)"
  DEPS="$(json_get "$MANABS" depends_on)"; INVARIANTS="$(json_get "$MANABS" invariants)"; SCOPE="$(json_get "$MANABS" scope)"
  BASE_REF="$(json_get "$MANABS" base_ref)"; [ -n "$BASE_REF" ] || BASE_REF="main"
  MANIFEST_SCHEMA_VERSION="$(json_get "$MANABS" manifest_schema_version)"
  REVIEW_GATE_VERSION="$(json_get "$MANABS" review_gate_version)"
  DEP_CHECKS="$(json_get_dep_checks "$MANABS")"
  [ -n "$FEATURE" ] || halt_gate CONTRACT_INVALID "manifest missing 'feature'"
  if command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    MANREL="$(python3 - "$ROOT" "$MANABS" <<'PY'
import os,sys;print(os.path.relpath(sys.argv[2],sys.argv[1]))
PY
)"
  else MANREL="${MANABS#"$ROOT"/}"; fi
  # invariant catalog: normally docs/autopilot-invariant-catalog.md (sibling of the manifests dir).
  # v6 FIX: a RUNTIME/TEMP manifest may live OUTSIDE docs/ (e.g. .autopilot/state/<feature>/manifest.json
  # so no untracked file lands in docs/). The sibling derivation then resolves to the wrong dir and
  # `register` HALTs INVARIANT_UNKNOWN even though the catalog exists. Resolve in order:
  #   explicit $AUTOPILOT_INVARIANT_CATALOG > sibling-of-manifest > worktree docs/ > MAIN repo docs/.
  _cat_dir="$(cd "$(dirname "$manifest")/.." 2>/dev/null && pwd)" || _cat_dir=""
  CATALOG=""
  for _c in "${AUTOPILOT_INVARIANT_CATALOG:-}" \
            "${_cat_dir:+$_cat_dir/autopilot-invariant-catalog.md}" \
            "$WORKTREE_ROOT/docs/autopilot-invariant-catalog.md" \
            "$ROOT/docs/autopilot-invariant-catalog.md"; do
    if [ -n "$_c" ] && [ -f "$_c" ]; then CATALOG="$_c"; break; fi
  done
  [ -n "$CATALOG" ] || CATALOG="${_cat_dir:+$_cat_dir/}autopilot-invariant-catalog.md"
else
  FEATURE="$feature_arg"
  case "$FEATURE" in
    ""|[!a-z0-9]*|*[!a-z0-9._-]*) ;;
    *) TASK_STATE_CANDIDATE="$ROOT/.autopilot/state/$FEATURE" ;;
  esac
fi

# canonical invariant tokens between the catalog markers. The catalog contract is one token as the
# first whitespace-delimited field per line. Avoid a sed character class containing an em dash: BSD
# sed can parse `[—-]` as an invalid/locale-sensitive range and silently return an empty catalog.
catalog_tokens(){
  [ -f "$CATALOG" ] || return 1
  awk '
    $0 == "<!-- catalog:start -->"{starts++; if(!start_line)start_line=NR; c=1; next}
    $0 == "<!-- catalog:end -->"{ends++; if(!end_line)end_line=NR; c=0; next}
    c && $1 ~ /^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*$/ {print $1}
    END {if (!(starts == 1 && ends == 1 && start_line < end_line)) exit 1}
  ' "$CATALOG" | sort -u
}

# --- portable atomic lock (mkdir) ---
acquire(){ local t=0; until mkdir "$LOCK" 2>/dev/null; do sleep 0.2; t=$((t+1)); [ $t -gt 150 ] && halt_gate REGISTRY_LOCK_TIMEOUT "shared registry lock did not clear: $LOCK"; done; REGISTRY_LOCK_HELD=1; }
release(){ rmdir "$LOCK" 2>/dev/null || true; REGISTRY_LOCK_HELD=0; }
acquire_ready(){
  local t=0
  READY_LOCK="$DIR/READY-$FEATURE.lock"
  until mkdir "$READY_LOCK" 2>/dev/null; do
    sleep 0.2
    t=$((t+1))
    if [ $t -gt 150 ]; then
      # This rejects a duplicate invocation; it must not poison the valid transition that owns the
      # lock with a HALT file.
      echo "REJECT READY_TRANSITION_BUSY: another readiness transition for '$FEATURE' is still running." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
  done
  READY_LOCK_HELD=1
}
release_ready(){ rmdir "$READY_LOCK" 2>/dev/null || true; READY_LOCK_HELD=0; }

# lines (scope|invariants values) for every ACTIVE (in-flight|ready) feature != me
active_others(){ # $1 = scope|invariants
  awk -v me="$FEATURE" -v want="$1" '
    /^```/{f=!f;next} f{next}
    /^## feature:/{cur=$3;st="";sec=""}
    /^status:/{st=$2}
    /^invariants:/{sec="invariants";next}
    /^scope:/{sec="scope";next}
    /^## /{sec=""}
    sec==want && /^- / && (st=="in-flight"||st=="ready") && cur!=me {print $2}
  ' "$INFLIGHT" | sort -u; }

active_feature_names(){ awk '/^```/{f=!f;next} f{next} /^## feature:/{cur=$3} /^status:/{if(($2=="in-flight"||$2=="ready"))print cur}' "$INFLIGHT" | sort -u; }

drop_my_block(){ awk -v me="$FEATURE" '
    /^```/{f=!f} !f && /^## feature:/{drop=($3==me)} !drop{print}
  ' "$INFLIGHT" > "$INFLIGHT.tmp.$$" && mv "$INFLIGHT.tmp.$$" "$INFLIGHT"; }

append_block(){ # $1 = status
  { printf '\n## feature: %s\nbranch: %s\nstatus: %s\nrisk: %s\nmanifest: %s\n' "$FEATURE" "$BRANCH" "$1" "$RISK" "$MANREL"
    if [ -n "$DEPS" ]; then printf 'depends_on:\n'; printf '%s\n' "$DEPS" | sed 's/^/- /'; fi
    printf 'invariants:\n'; printf '%s\n' "$INVARIANTS" | sed 's/^/- /'
    printf 'scope:\n'; printf '%s\n' "$SCOPE" | sed 's/^/- /'
  } >> "$INFLIGHT"; }

case "$cmd" in
  register)
    # v28 (defect 7): register must not exit 0 without locking the manifest. Checked up front,
    # before acquire/append_block touch INFLIGHT.md or TASK_STATE is created, so a manifest that
    # cannot be locked leaves no dangling reservation for a corrected retry to collide with.
    [ "$MANIFEST_SCHEMA_VERSION" = "level3-operational-v2" ] || halt_gate CONTRACT_INVALID \
      "manifest '$MANABS' is missing manifest_schema_version=level3-operational-v2 (got '${MANIFEST_SCHEMA_VERSION:-<empty>}'); register only locks the runtime manifest at .autopilot/state/$FEATURE/manifest.json — the authored contract under docs/autopilot-manifests/ must never carry this field and is not a valid --manifest input to register"
    REVIEW_GATE="$WORKTREE_ROOT/scripts/autopilot-review-readiness.mjs"
    if [ "$MANIFEST_SCHEMA_VERSION" = "level3-operational-v2" ] || [ -n "$REVIEW_GATE_VERSION" ]; then
      [ -f "$REVIEW_GATE" ] || halt_gate REVIEW_GATE_MISSING "$REVIEW_GATE"
      node "$REVIEW_GATE" register --manifest "$MANABS"
    fi
    acquire
    [ -f "$INFLIGHT" ] || printf '# Autopilot in-flight registry\n\n---\n' > "$INFLIGHT"
    if active_feature_names | grep -qxF "$FEATURE"; then
      # Registration did not admit a new lifecycle. Reject the duplicate without terminalizing the
      # already-valid active lifecycle.
      echo "REJECT FEATURE_ALREADY_ACTIVE: '$FEATURE' already has an in-flight/ready reservation." >&2
      echo "  → resume the exact existing contract without re-registering, or use a fresh task-scoped feature id." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    # (0) invariant catalog check (#22): every manifest invariant MUST be a canonical token.
    cat_tokens="$(catalog_tokens)" \
      || halt_gate INVARIANT_UNKNOWN "catalog missing/malformed; tried explicit, manifest sibling, worktree docs and root docs; last: $CATALOG"
    if [ -n "$INVARIANTS" ]; then
      unknown="$(comm -23 <(printf '%s\n' "$INVARIANTS" | sort -u) <(printf '%s\n' "$cat_tokens"))"
      if [ -n "$unknown" ]; then
        halt_gate INVARIANT_UNKNOWN "'$FEATURE' uses token(s) absent from the catalog: $(printf '%s' "$unknown" | tr '\n' ',')"
      fi
    fi
    # (a) Runtime dependency check. The v2 validator already proved each dependency landed on the
    # pinned base; an active reservation still blocks to prevent racing a not-yet-disposed slice.
    if [ -n "$DEPS" ]; then
      act="$(active_feature_names)"
      while IFS= read -r d; do [ -z "$d" ] && continue
        if printf '%s\n' "$act" | grep -qxF "$d"; then
          halt_gate DEP_MISSING "prerequisite '$d' is still active (in-flight|ready), not landed on the pinned base"
        fi
      done <<< "$DEPS"
    fi
    # (a2) Fast interface-presence check. The v2 validator additionally proves unique-symbol
    # absence on the dependency base and presence at landed_sha + downstream base_sha.
    if [ -n "$DEP_CHECKS" ]; then
      git rev-parse --verify -q "$BASE_REF^{commit}" >/dev/null \
        || halt_gate DEP_MISSING "base_ref '$BASE_REF' not found locally (git fetch first)"
      while IFS="$(printf '\t')" read -r f sym; do [ -z "$f" ] && continue
        blob="$(git show "$BASE_REF:$f" 2>/dev/null)" \
          || halt_gate DEP_MISSING "'$FEATURE' expects '$f' on $BASE_REF but the file is absent"
        grep -qF -- "$sym" <<< "$blob" \
          || halt_gate DEP_MISSING "symbol '$sym' absent from $BASE_REF:$f; re-derive from landed code"
      done <<< "$DEP_CHECKS"
    fi
    # (b) collision check: scope file OR invariant intersects another active feature
    cf="$(comm -12 <(printf '%s\n' "$SCOPE" | sort -u) <(active_others scope))"
    ci="$(comm -12 <(printf '%s\n' "$INVARIANTS" | sort -u) <(active_others invariants))"
    if [ -n "$cf$ci" ]; then
      halt_gate SCOPE_COLLISION "'$FEATURE' intersects active scope/invariants; files=$(printf '%s' "$cf" | tr '\n' ',') invariants=$(printf '%s' "$ci" | tr '\n' ',')"
    fi
    drop_my_block; append_block in-flight
    # Step-1 state is mechanical, not prompt discipline. Preserve existing counters on resume,
    # but guarantee both breaker files exist before implementation or review begins.
    TASK_STATE="$WORKTREE_ROOT/.autopilot/state/$FEATURE"
    mkdir -p "$TASK_STATE"
    [ -f "$TASK_STATE/fix-round-count.txt" ] || printf '0\n' > "$TASK_STATE/fix-round-count.txt"
    [ -f "$TASK_STATE/regions.log" ] || : > "$TASK_STATE/regions.log"
    if [ "$MANIFEST_SCHEMA_VERSION" = "level3-operational-v2" ]; then
      node -e 'const fs=require("fs"),c=require("crypto");const b=fs.readFileSync(process.argv[1]);process.stdout.write(c.createHash("sha256").update(b).digest("hex")+"\n")' \
        "$MANABS" > "$TASK_STATE/registered-manifest.sha256"
    fi
    echo "registered: $FEATURE (in-flight)"
    ;;
  ready)
    # A ready lifecycle is terminal. Refuse a second ready attempt before the validator can remove
    # READY.txt/gate-result.json, otherwise the registry could remain `ready` while its proof vanishes.
    acquire_ready
    acquire
    [ -f "$INFLIGHT" ] || printf '# Autopilot in-flight registry\n\n---\n' > "$INFLIGHT"
    me_status="$(awk -v me="$FEATURE" '
      # No reset on the header line: this awk accumulates ONE named feature and prints at END, so
      # clearing on every later header erases the answer unless me is the LAST block in the registry.
      # The per-block scanner further up DOES reset, correctly, because it evaluates each block in turn.
      # Same idiom, opposite requirement. Rationale in the v20 header note.
      /^## feature:/{cur=$3}
      /^status:/{if(cur==me)st=$2}
      END{print st}
    ' "$INFLIGHT")"
    if [ "$me_status" != "in-flight" ]; then
      # A terminal READY already has its signal; an absent lifecycle was never admitted. Reject
      # either invalid invocation without creating a conflicting HALT file.
      echo "REJECT READY_LIFECYCLE_INVALID: '$FEATURE' status is '${me_status:-absent}', expected in-flight." >&2
      echo "  → never re-run ready on a terminal lifecycle; create a fresh successor task when more work is required." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    release
    # Run the potentially long verify + Codex gate BEFORE taking the shared registry lock. The gate
    # binds its attestation to HEAD + current base SHA + diff hash; ap-finish verifies it again.
    REVIEW_GATE="$WORKTREE_ROOT/scripts/autopilot-review-readiness.mjs"
    REVIEW_REQUIRED=0
    [ "$MANIFEST_SCHEMA_VERSION" = "level3-operational-v2" ] && REVIEW_REQUIRED=1
    [ -n "$REVIEW_GATE_VERSION" ] && REVIEW_REQUIRED=1
    git cat-file -e "$BASE_REF:scripts/autopilot-review-readiness.mjs" 2>/dev/null && REVIEW_REQUIRED=1 || true
    if [ "$REVIEW_REQUIRED" = 1 ] && [ -f "$REVIEW_GATE" ]; then
      node "$REVIEW_GATE" ready --manifest "$MANABS"
    elif [ "$REVIEW_REQUIRED" = 1 ]; then
      halt_gate REVIEW_GATE_MISSING "$REVIEW_GATE"
    else
      echo "WARN: legacy manifest without automatic review-readiness; applying registry-only transition" >&2
    fi
    acquire
    [ -f "$INFLIGHT" ] || printf '# Autopilot in-flight registry\n\n---\n' > "$INFLIGHT"
    # Require a prior active block: ready must follow a successful register.
    me_status="$(awk -v me="$FEATURE" '
      # No reset on the header line: this awk accumulates ONE named feature and prints at END, so
      # clearing on every later header erases the answer unless me is the LAST block in the registry.
      # The per-block scanner further up DOES reset, correctly, because it evaluates each block in turn.
      # Same idiom, opposite requirement. Rationale in the v20 header note.
      /^## feature:/{cur=$3}
      /^status:/{if(cur==me)st=$2}
      END{print st}
    ' "$INFLIGHT")"
    if [ "$me_status" != "in-flight" ]; then
      halt_gate READY_LIFECYCLE_CHANGED "'$FEATURE' changed to '${me_status:-absent}' while readiness was running; gate result is non-authoritative"
    fi
    # Re-check collisions: another feature may have registered between our register and ready calls.
    cf="$(comm -12 <(printf '%s\n' "$SCOPE" | sort -u) <(active_others scope))"
    ci="$(comm -12 <(printf '%s\n' "$INVARIANTS" | sort -u) <(active_others invariants))"
    if [ -n "$cf$ci" ]; then
      halt_gate SCOPE_COLLISION "'$FEATURE' acquired a collision while readiness ran; files=$(printf '%s' "$cf" | tr '\n' ',') invariants=$(printf '%s' "$ci" | tr '\n' ',')"
    fi
    drop_my_block
    append_block ready
    TASK_STATE="$WORKTREE_ROOT/.autopilot/state/$FEATURE"
    mkdir -p "$TASK_STATE"
    printf 'READY: readiness gate passed for %s\n' "$FEATURE" > "$TASK_STATE/READY.txt"
    release
    release_ready
    echo "ready: $FEATURE" ;;
  cleanup)
    RECORD=""
    [ -s "$ROOT/.autopilot/landings/$FEATURE.json" ] \
      && RECORD="$ROOT/.autopilot/landings/$FEATURE.json"
    [ -z "$RECORD" ] && [ -s "$ROOT/.autopilot/dispositions/$FEATURE.json" ] \
      && RECORD="$ROOT/.autopilot/dispositions/$FEATURE.json"
    [ -n "$RECORD" ] \
      || { echo "REJECT CLEANUP_PROOF_MISSING: use scripts/ap-finish.sh; raw reservation cleanup is forbidden." >&2; exit 1; }
    command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || { echo "REJECT CLEANUP_PROOF_INVALID: node is required to validate $RECORD" >&2; exit 1; }
    if ! node - "$RECORD" "$FEATURE" <<'NODE'
const fs = require("fs");
const [file, feature] = process.argv.slice(2);
const value = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(file, "utf8"));
if (
  value.schema_version !== "level3-disposition-v1"
  || value.feature !== feature
  || !["landed", "authorized_close"].includes(value.status)
) process.exit(1);
NODE
    then
      echo "REJECT CLEANUP_PROOF_INVALID: $RECORD" >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    acquire
    [ -f "$INFLIGHT" ] || printf '# Autopilot in-flight registry\n\n---\n' > "$INFLIGHT"
    drop_my_block; echo "cleaned: $FEATURE" ;;
  *) echo "usage: autopilot-scope-gate <register|ready|cleanup> --manifest <path>   (or: cleanup --feature <slug>)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac
